---
// The intro's "same grid, different burden" comparison as two supply chains.
---

<div class="chain" role="group" aria-label="Two supply chains compared">
  <div class="chain-col" data-tone="fuel">
    <p class="eyebrow">The fuel chain</p>
    <h3>Dig. Ship. Refine. Burn. Repeat.</h3>
    <ol class="chain-steps" aria-label="Steps in the fuel chain">
      <li class="chain-step">Dig</li>
      <li class="chain-step">Ship</li>
      <li class="chain-step">Refine</li>
      <li class="chain-step">Burn</li>
      <li class="chain-step loop">Repeat, forever</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Every round trip needs routes, contracts, price luck, waste handling, and a lot of political protection. The
      chain never stops asking.
    </p>
    <div class="chain-meter">
      <span>Fuel purchases</span>
      <b>Every day the plant runs</b>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="chain-col" data-tone="sun">
    <p class="eyebrow">The panel</p>
    <h3>Build. Connect. Maintain. Generate.</h3>
    <ol class="chain-steps" aria-label="Steps in the solar chain">
      <li class="chain-step">Build</li>
      <li class="chain-step">Connect</li>
      <li class="chain-step">Maintain</li>
      <li class="chain-step end">Generate, ~30 years</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Solar has real costs: hardware, land, wire, batteries for the evening. But once the panel is up, the fuel doesn't
      need a train, a tanker, a mine, or a cartel.
    </p>
    <div class="chain-meter">
      <span>Fuel purchases</span>
      <b>Zero</b>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
